Now perform the same with the following input
input: {
    "context": "Able and available to work – If you said you were not able or available to work when you certified, you may not be eligible for benefits that week. Even if you are not eligible for benefits one week, you still may be eligible other weeks, so you should continue to certify.\nEarnings – If you earned too much in a week, you may not be eligible for benefits that week. When certifying for regular unemployment benefits, you must report total earnings for the week you worked. The first $25 or 25% of your wages, whichever is more, will not be deducted from your benefit amount, but the rest will.\nSuitable work – If you said you refused any work when you certified, we must schedule a phone interview to ask why. We will also contact the employer that offered you the job. If you refused to return to your job or other suitable work without good cause, you may not be eligible for benefits. To learn more about good cause, refer to Returning to Work.\nWas this helpful?\n\nYes\n\nNo\n\ndid this information help you with your case?\n\nGreat! Anything you can share about what made it helpful?\n\nSorry to hear that! What would make this more helpful?\n\nAny Additional Feedback?\n\nEmail (optional)\n\n CAPTCHA\nThis question is for testing whether or not you are a human visitor and to prevent automated spam submissions.\n\nLeave this field blank\n\n Was this helpful?\n\nsuccess alert banner:\nHave a question about Civil lawsuits?\nLook for a \"Chat Now\" button in the right bottom corner of your screen. If you don’t see it, disable any pop-up/ad blockers on your browser.\n\nCALIFORNIA COURTS | SELF HELP GUIDE\n\n \n\n \n\n Judicial Branch of California\n\nTerms of Use\n\nPrivacy Policy\n\n ©\n 2024\nfor benefits, a claimant must be able to work, be seeking work, and be willing to accept a suitable job.\nThe UI program is a federal-state program, authorized in federal law but with broad discretion for states to set benefit and employer contribution levels. The\r\nprogram is financed by unemployment tax contributions paid by employers for each covered worker. We note that California law allows a part-time employee\r\nto file a UI claim.\nStatutory Benefit Level. State law establishes benefit levels. Currently, the maximum weekly benefit is capped by state law at $230 per week for 26 weeks. The\r\namount of benefits available is based on the claimant's earnings in the \"base period.\"",
    "statements": [
        "The Unemployment Insurance program is a federal-state program authorized by federal law.",
        "State governments set unemployment benefit levels.",
        "State governments set employer contribution levels.",
        "Employer unemployment taxes finance the Unemployment Insurance program.",
        "An unemployment insurance claimant must be able to work to qualify for benefits.",
        "An unemployment insurance claimant must seek work to qualify for benefits.",
        "An unemployment insurance claimant must be willing to accept suitable work to qualify for benefits.",
        "California allows part-time employees to file unemployment insurance claims.",
        "Unemployment insurance benefits depend on claimant earnings during the base period.",
        "California provides a maximum unemployment insurance benefit of $230 weekly for 26 weeks.",
        "An unemployment insurance claimant must report total weekly earnings during weekly certification.",
        "The greater amount between $25 and 25 percent of wages is not deducted from weekly unemployment insurance benefits.",
        "Weekly earnings exceeding the exempt amount are deducted from unemployment insurance benefits.",
        "Excess earnings during a week may make an unemployment insurance claimant ineligible for benefits during the week.",
        "Inability to work during a week may make an unemployment insurance claimant ineligible for benefits during the week.",
        "Unavailability for work during a week may make an unemployment insurance claimant ineligible for benefits during the week.",
        "An unemployment insurance claimant should continue weekly certification for other eligible weeks.",
        "Refusal of suitable work triggers a phone interview.",
        "Refusal of suitable work triggers contact with the employer.",
        "Refusal of suitable work without good cause may affect unemployment insurance eligibility."
    ]
}
